          "session_id": "full-g-md-005",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My HbA1c came back at 7.2%. What does that mean, what target does guidance describe, and which code would type 2 diabetes be recorded under?",
          "answer": "Your HbA1c of 7.2% reflects your average blood glucose over roughly the previous three months. The sources don't explain what this result means for your own health, so ask your clinician how it compares with the ranges they use and with your own history.\n\nThe sources differ on the target figure. The guidance *Type 2 diabetes: glycemic targets and monitoring intervals* was described by one source as giving a reasonable goal of below 7% for many non-pregnant adults, to reduce small-vessel problems in the eyes, kidneys and nerves. It said a stricter goal, below 6.5%, may suit some people if it can be reached without significant low blood sugar. The other source could not confirm that figure from the passages it retrieved. It said the standard goal is stricter than 8%, and that a less stringent goal below 8% may be set for people with a history of severe low blood sugar, limited life expectancy, advanced complications, or a target that has proved hard to reach. The targets guidance is also reported to contain a disagreement between authorities, which I could not retrieve, so I can't say how they differ. Your clinician can tell you which target applies to you.\n\nGuidance says HbA1c is checked at least twice a year when you are at goal, and about every three months when you are not at goal or when treatment has recently changed.\n\nType 2 diabetes is recorded as **E11** in ICD-10-CM, the US clinical modification, in Chapter 4 (endocrine, nutritional and metabolic diseases). If a record says only \"diabetes mellitus\" without a type, the convention is to default to type 2. Taking insulin does not change the code, although a separate status code records long-term insulin use. Other countries' ICD-10 versions may differ, and code sets are updated yearly, so check the version your record uses.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
          "wall_ms": 19514.750897000114,
